          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"strindberg-froken-julie-1492b64f21186bd3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":3,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"The embedded clause “jag älskar dig” represents the proposition that Julie has loving feelings for Jean, giving it strong truth-conditional support. However, Julie embeds it under “du tror att” (“you think that”) and voices it as Jean’s alleged belief rather than avowing it herself. It undertakes no relational commitment, and the surrounding emotional violence does not independently make the love clause an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is whether to treat the embedded love proposition as fully T despite Julie’s distancing and apparent repudiation; these affect assertion and attribution, not the clause’s love-state content.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Julie directly says “you think that I love you,” revoicing and challenging a belief she attributes to Jean; this is not a direct declaration of love by Julie.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After Jean kills Julie’s bird, Julie launches into a violent denunciation. She casts “I love you” as Jean’s mistaken inference from their sexual encounter and from reproductive desire, then counters it with fantasies of violence, class contempt, and rejection of marriage and motherhood. The highly agitated delivery intensifies her repudiation but does not turn the embedded love proposition into an expressive discharge or commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Du tror att jag älskar dig” (“You think that I love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The love-state proposition is embedded under an attribution of belief to Jean rather than asserted as Julie’s own avowal; this supports T while qualifying its strength and establishes revoicing.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"E2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Julie immediately explains Jean’s supposed inference as arising “därför att min livsfrukt åtrådde ditt frö” and derides bearing his child and taking his name.\",\"supports\":\"Julie distances herself from the attributed love and rejects the relational and reproductive future associated with it, providing no performative undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"E3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The clause occurs amid Julie’s declarations that she hates and loathes Jean and her violent fantasies concerning his blood and body.\",\"supports\":\"The scene frames the attributed love as repudiated or contested; its emotional intensity is contextual delivery evidence, not independent E force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"E4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense embedded Swedish form in August Strindberg’s Fröken Julie, at relative position 0.808392.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the target is an embedded equivalent of “I love you” within dramatic dialogue.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the represented loving-state proposition, while the status and contextual account capture its attribution to Jean and Julie’s repudiation. P and E lack independent support, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The translation was treated only as an analytical aid; the classification is grounded in the supplied Swedish source text.\"}"
        }
